                <h2>The Scene: A 'Viral Creative' That Died a Strange Death</h2>
<p>Last Thursday evening, Xiao Lin, an optimizer at a home goods cross-border team, posted in the review group: "Help! This video creative has a 9.2% CTR and a CPC of just $0.28, but after running for 6 days and spending $22, zero conversions."</p>
<p>The group exploded. Someone asked if the audience targeting was wrong, another suspected landing page loading issues, and someone else said flatly: "This creative sucks, stop running it."</p>
<p>After a full investigation, Xiao Lin found: landing page was fine, payment flow was normal, even the pixel was correctly installed. But the creative itself only showcased the product appearance and usage scenarios, with zero messaging around "why buy this" or "why buy now." <strong>It could run, but it couldn't sell.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Core Issue: Are You Optimizing for 'Running Metrics' or 'Selling Metrics'?</h2>
<p>Meta's ad dashboard provides tons of data, and optimizers often fall into two traps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Only looking at CTR and CPC</strong>, assuming good-looking numbers mean good creatives</li>
<li><strong>Only focusing on conversion counts and ROAS</strong>, but unable to find optimization direction</li>
</ol>
<p>In reality, creative review needs to separate metrics into two categories:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Running Metrics (Acquisition Power)</th>
<th>Selling Metrics (Conversion Power)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR (Click-Through Rate)</td>
<td>CVR (Conversion Rate)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPC (Cost Per Click)</td>
<td>CPA (Cost Per Acquisition)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Reach (Audience Reach)</td>
<td>ROAS (Return on Ad Spend)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Video Completion Rate</td>
<td>Add-to-Cart Rate, Checkout Rate</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Running creatives</strong> have excellent traffic entrance, pulling people in. <strong>Selling creatives</strong> complete the final push from "interested" to "purchased." The ideal scenario balances both, but if you had to choose, which would you prioritize?</p>
<hr />
<h2>Judgment Method: 4-Step Quick Screening for Creative Attributes</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Pull the Timeline and Check the CTR Curve</h3>
<p>A continuously rising CTR means the algorithm is learning the target audience. But if CVR is simultaneously dropping, the creative might be attracting the wrong people. For example, a $50 massage gun creative using "extreme sports injury" as the hook has high clicks, but the actual target user is "sedentary office workers"—natural conversion is poor.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Compare Creative Type with Landing Page Relevance</h3>
<p>A feed creative uses price anchoring + discount info to guide clicks, but the landing page is still talking about product features. Users click in, realize "this isn't what I expected," and bounce immediately. This is the classic signature of "can run but can't sell": <strong>high clicks + high bounce rate + low dwell time</strong>.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Identify Where the Conversion Funnel Leaks</h3>
<p>Go to Meta Ads Manager's funnel report and break down "click → add to cart → checkout → payment" conversion rates at each layer. If add-to-cart rate is below 3%, the problem is likely that the creative didn't create enough purchase urgency. If add-to-cart is high but checkout is low, the landing page has pricing or trust issues.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Trace Creative Elements and SKU Match</h3>
<p>The same backpack, a travel scenario creative versus a commute scenario creative can have 3x difference in CVR. During review, tag creatives by "usage scenario / persona / pain point" and cross-analyze with converted SKUs. You'll discover your truly sellable creatives are actually from one specific category.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Plan: Creative Reactivation Strategy</h2>
<p>Back to Xiao Lin's case—after identifying the problem, he did three things:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Added urgency hook</strong>: Inserted "Limited 30% Off + Free Shipping" text overlay in the first 3 seconds of the video</li>
<li><strong>Modified CTA path</strong>: Changed from "Buy Now" to "View Discount Code," lowering first-layer decision cost</li>
<li><strong>Split audience test</strong>: Divided the original broad audience into two versions by interest: "Home Decor" and "DIY Enthusiast"</li>
</ol>
<p>One week later, the same creative's CVR jumped from 0.8% to 2.3%, and CPA went from "incalculable" to $12. Though CTR slightly dropped to 7.1%, overall ROAS reached 3.2. <strong>It went from running to selling.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Takeaway: Ask Yourself These Three Questions Before Every Creative Review</h2>
<ol>
<li>Am I looking at "traffic metrics" or "conversion metrics"? Are both meeting targets simultaneously?</li>
<li>How much overlap is there between users who clicked on this creative and users who actually purchased?</li>
<li>If CTR is high but CVR is low, should I prioritize adjusting the creative hook or the landing page?</li>
</ol>
<p>Running creatives are your ticket; selling creatives are your revenue. Next time you review, don't let CTR fool you.</p>
